<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/intermediate/close-reading">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The foundational tier taught you to follow a story — plot, character, setting, theme.
    Close reading is the skill underneath all of that: <strong>reading at word-and-sentence
    level to see how a text makes its effects</strong>. The New Critics (Brooks, Warren,
    Empson) built the discipline in the 1930s–40s on a radical claim: the evidence for any
    interpretation lives in the words on the page, and careful attention to those words
    outranks biographical gossip or guesses about what the author "really meant".
  </p>
  <p>
    The method earns its keep twice. First, it is what literature assessment actually
    rewards: every board's rubric asks for understanding of <em>how</em> writers use language
    and structure to achieve effects — which is this skill, named. Second, it is honest.
    Anyone can assert that a poem is "about grief". Close reading makes you show which words
    do the grieving and how. An interpretation without a sentence-level case is an opinion;
    one with it is an argument.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four instruments: diction, imagery, syntax, repetition" widget="equationStep" config={closeCfg} />

  <h2>The method: how to read an unseen extract</h2>
  <p>
    Close reading of set texts, unseen texts and short extracts is one skill at three
    temperatures. With a set text you already know the whole; with an unseen extract you are
    starting cold. Either way the procedure is the same, and it has steps.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>First pass — gist.</strong> Read once for what happens and who is speaking. Note the form (sonnet, dramatic monologue, novel opening) and the situation. Do not annotate yet; you cannot weigh words before you know what the passage is doing.</li>
    <li><strong>Second pass — language.</strong> Read again slowly and mark everything deliberate: striking word choices, repetitions, images, a change of sentence length, a turn in the argument. If you notice it, an examiner will reward your explaining it.</li>
    <li><strong>Pattern pass — structure.</strong> Ask how the passage moves. Where does it turn? What is set up and what pays off? In a 14-line poem the turn is often at line 9 or 13; in prose, look for the sentence where the register shifts.</li>
    <li><strong>Then zoom out.</strong> The local patterns become the global claim: the image cluster you tracked becomes your thesis about the poem. Close reading serves interpretation, never replaces it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worked miniature. Take Dickinson: <em>"Because I could not stop for Death — / He
    kindly stopped for me —"</em>. First pass: a woman rides in a carriage with Death. Second
    pass: the diction ("kindly") is the shock — Death as courteous suitor, not horror; the
    dashes enact hesitation; "stopped" repeats, first as her refusal, then as his courtesy.
    Pattern pass: the whole poem is structured as a journey with stages, so form and content
    agree. Every claim here comes from words visible on the page — that is the discipline.
  </p>

  <h2>Diction: the word-level case</h2>
  <p>
    Diction is word choice, and it is where analysis usually starts because it is where the
    writer's decisions are most visible. Two tools do most of the work. The first is the
    <strong>connotation test</strong>: "slender" and "scrawny" denote similar size but connote
    opposite judgments; "kids", "children", "offspring" and "brats" are four attitudes in
    four nouns. When you find a striking word, generate its near-synonyms and ask what the
    writer's actual choice has that the alternatives lack. That difference <em>is</em> the
    effect.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second tool is <strong>register</strong> — the social level of the vocabulary. English
    keeps a layered wardrobe: Anglo-Saxon short words (fear, grief, house, love) sit close to
    the body; Latinate longer words (apprehension, edifice, affection) sit at a distance of
    formality or abstraction. A writer who switches layers mid-passage is usually doing
    something: a politician who says "collateral damage" has buried bodies under Latin. Watch
    especially for the sudden plain word after fancy ones, or vice versa — the collision is
    the meaning.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> diction analysis is not a hunt for "difficult" or "fancy"
  words. The most important choices are often tiny — a preposition, an adjective, a pronoun
  — and examiners regularly build mark schemes around them. "Kindly" is an ordinary word;
  in that line it is the whole poem.</p>

  <h2>Imagery: the pattern that argues</h2>
  <p>
    Imagery is language that appeals to the senses, but the analytical unit is not the single
    image — it is the <strong>cluster</strong>. When a poem about grief keeps showing weather,
    the weather is the argument: it carries the emotional logic the surface sentences do not
    state. So track images the way you would track a motif in music: note each one, then ask
    how it changes on each return.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked case. In Macbeth, images of disease, blood and darkness arrive in waves, and
    critics have counted them: "sleep" alone is mentioned over thirty times across the play,
    and nearly always as something murdered, lost or poisoned — Macbeth "murders sleep" the
    night he kills Duncan. The pattern argues what no single line states: that regicide is a
    sickness infecting the whole body of Scotland. In Dickinson's carriage poem, the images
    are of a journey past children, fields and a setting sun — a life reviewed at speed.
    Neither poet ever announces the theme; the images announce it.
  </p>
  <p>
    For each image you can ask the classical pair of questions: what is the <strong>tenor</strong>
    (the thing actually meant) and the <strong>vehicle</strong> (the thing it is pictured as)?
    In "Hope is the thing with feathers", hope is tenor, bird is vehicle — and the poem's
    details (it "perches", it sings "without the words") are all properties transferred from
    vehicle to tenor. Good image analysis names which property is doing the work.
  </p>

  <h2>The sentence is the argument</h2>
  <p>
    Syntax — the shape of sentences — performs meaning. Long balanced sentences enact
    balance; fragments enact shock; a sentence that withholds its main verb builds suspense.
    The classic pairing: Faulkner wrote endurance-length sentences (one sentence in
    <em>Absalom, Absalom!</em> runs past a thousand words) while Hemingway built his style
    from short declaratives that enact restraint and refusal. Neither is "better writing" —
    each is an argument about experience, made in grammar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practical sub-skills: notice <strong>sentence length changes</strong> (a short sentence
    after long ones lands like a hammer), <strong>coordination vs subordination</strong>
    ("and… and… and…" mimics accumulation; "although… yet…" mimics struggle), and
    <strong>fronted elements</strong> — whatever a sentence opens with is being stressed.
    "Never give all the heart" opens stressed because the command is urgent. In prose, a
    sentence that opens with the object rather than the subject has made a small, readable
    decision worth noting.
  </p>

  <h2>Figures of speech and their purpose</h2>
  <p>
    The catalogue — metaphor, simile, personification, oxymoron and friends — gets its own
    lesson in this unit, so here just the <em>purpose</em> question, which is what examiners
    actually ask. Naming a figure earns almost nothing by itself; the question is what the
    figure accomplishes that plain statement could not. Personification in "Death kindly
    stopped" is not decoration: it is the poem's whole theology, making death a gentleman
    caller so the poem can dramatise acceptance instead of terror. Oxymoron ("O brawling
    love, O loving hate") is not a paradox for display: it stages the speaker's divided
    state in a single breath. Rule of thumb: name the figure, then spend the rest of the
    sentence on its job.
  </p>

  <h2>From reading to essay: structure and quotation</h2>
  <p>
    Close reading pays off on the page in essays, and the bridge is a repeatable paragraph
    shape: <strong>claim → evidence → analysis → link</strong>. Open with a point about
    method (not plot): "Dickinson makes Death courteous through her diction." Supply the
    quotation. Then analyse — this is where most of the marks live — naming the specific
    words and their effect. Finally link back to the thesis. A whole essay is six to eight
    of these units aimed at one argument.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quotation mechanics matter because they are visible craft. Embed short quotations inside
    your own sentence ("the verb 'stopped' repeats…") rather than dumping long blocks. In
    verse, mark a line break with a slash: "he kindly stopped for me — / The Carriage held…".
    Use square brackets for any alteration: "it "[passes] us" shows…". Give line or act-scene
    references in parentheses. And quote precisely: an examiner checks, and a misquoted word
    costs credibility that no cleverness buys back.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> more quotation is not more evidence. A paragraph built of
  five quotations and two sentences of commentary reads as summary. One quotation, with its
  diction opened up, is stronger every time.</p>

  <h2>Repetition: the cheapest signal, the loudest</h2>
  <p>
    Before the finer instruments, use the coarsest one: track what returns. Repetition is
    never accidental, because every printed repetition cost the author a decision. Anaphora
    ("I have a dream…") builds insistence; a recurring image builds a motif; a repeated
    phrase in a new context builds irony. The method takes seconds and pays immediately:
    circle every repeated word in a passage, then read only those words in order. The
    sentence they make is the passage's skeleton.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked case: the opening of Dickens's Bleak House (1853) — "Fog everywhere. Fog up the
    river… fog down the river… fog in the Essex marshes, fog in the Kentish heights." The
    word repeats more than a dozen times in two paragraphs, each time climbing into a new
    institution (the Lord Chancellor, the court, the lawyers) until the fog is named as the
    Court of Chancery itself. No sentence states the argument "this institution is a fog";
    the repetition constructs it in front of you. That is the whole method in one example:
    notice, count, ask what the pattern argues.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The New Critics published two manifestos that still shape the rules. The
    <strong>intentional fallacy</strong> (Wimsatt and Beardsley, 1946) argues that the
    author's intention is unavailable as evidence — we have the words, not the mind — so
    claims must be built from the text. The <strong>affective fallacy</strong> (1949) warns
    against grading a work by its effects on the reader's tears. Both rules have modern
    caveats: we know more about authors' contexts than the New Critics did, and reader
    response is now a legitimate school. But the core discipline survives: whatever you
    claim, the text must carry it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further ideas deepen the method. William Empson's <em>Seven Types of Ambiguity</em>
    (1930) showed that the best lines hold several meanings at once, and that ambiguity is
    an achievement, not a fault — a pun is two arguments for the price of one word. And the
    Russian Formalist Viktor Shklovsky (1917) explained <em>why</em> literature slows us down:
    art exists to make the stone stony again, "defamiliarising" the habitual so we perceive
    rather than recognise. That is also the best one-sentence defence of close reading's
    speed tax.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the method has a quantitative vindication. When Mosteller and Wallace identified
    the author of the disputed Federalist Papers (1963), they did it with
    <strong>function words</strong> — the, of, upon — small words no style manual notices.
    Modern stylometry fingerprints authors the same way, and it works. Word-level attention,
    it turns out, is not a schoolroom ritual: it is how texts actually testify.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks, The Well Wrought Urn (1947); Empson, Seven Types of Ambiguity (1930); Wimsatt &amp; Beardsley, "The Intentional Fallacy" (1946); Mosteller &amp; Wallace, Inference and Disputed Authorship: The Federalist (1964); the Dickinson and Shakespeare texts themselves.</p>
